courses.learning_outcomes:
After taking this course, students will be able to:

1. explain how cultures function, and recognize the different perspectives that can be used to understand culture;
2. discuss how different cultures have both diverse and similar solutions to the problems of human existence;
3. analyze cultures and societies as informed through comparisons with other cultures and societies; and
4. explicate the connections among different subfields of social and cultural anthropology (which focus on different facets of culture, such as religion, gender, politics, economics, etc.) and how these facets are linked and integrated in each culture

By the end of this course, students will

1) understand how anthropology is practiced in its different areas and fields;
2) know the basics of how to write papers and how to most fruitfully proceed in their studies as anthropology students in the university;
3) know the department's different teachers and the different ways in which they envision anthropology; and

4) have a broad overall sense of what anthropology as a discipline consists of.

1) To learn field research techniques for ethnographic study;
2) To develop skills and knowledge in designing and carrying our research projects independently;
3) To enhance effective communication and writing abilities;
4) To be aware of ethical and personal issues of field research.

Students taking this course will:

learn to see the cultural nature of reality and of the supernatural;

understand how many apparently irrational and bizarre beliefs make sense in their cultural contexts;

learn how to use critical thinking while at the same time being tolerant of other views;

develop a healthy scientific skepticism, while at the same time remaining open to new ideas;

be able to analyze other cases that will emerge later in life.

Through taking this course, students will:

* understand how the treatment of death varies cross-culturally, and thus how our own personalunderstandings of death are not necessarily universally shared;
* analyze processes of grieving and structures of death ritual in terms of how they vary cross-culturally;
* apply theoretical schemes of cultural evolution to understandings of death over human history;
* evaluate how death and fear of death affects contemporary politics, economics, and culture in societies across the globe;
* evaluate the place of death within our own lives.

Students who have taken this course will:

* be able to see and understand the cultural and historical nature of sports;
* understand the roles that sports play in modern societies;
* know how to apply fieldwork methods to research a group’s sub-culture, including implicit and explicit rules;
* be able to analyze the elements of competition, cooperation, luck and skill, and other variables that make sports entertaining to play and watch and talk about;

be able to analyze and appreciate other sports that they will encounter later in life.

After the completion of this course, students will:

1. Understand and appreciate the cultural dimensions of business operations and organization in different historical, political, and social settings;
2. Acquire the necessary ethnographic skills to study diverse aspects of business operations and organization;
3. Be able to rethink and challenge the existing assumptions regarding business practices in the modern world;
4. Gain the analytical tools to examine issues of over-consumption, over-production, dispossession, oppression, and other relevant discontent under global capitalism;
5. Be able to examine critically the role SDGs could play in addressing disparities and injustices in the world;
6. Understand the importance of building a more inclusive, equal and diverse society.

Upon completing this course, students will be able to:

1. analyze the globalization of culture, a process taking place across the world today and shaping all of our lives;
2. recognize different aspects of the globalization of culture, including those involving recent history, economics, consumption, mass media, migration, and cultural identity;
3. discuss the costs and benefits of contemporary processes of globalization to individuals, societies, and the world; and
4. evaluate the nature of your own culture and selves within a globalizing world.

# 1 Understand what is “scientific reasoning”, and be able to evaluate claims about the past by combining anthropological knowledge and understanding of scientific techniques;

# 2 General understanding about the history of technology and basic knowledge background to appreciate technological achievements in the past;

# 3 Equip students with skills to conduct digital humanities research and foundation to develop cross-disciplinary study;

# 4 Inspire students of different backgrounds to employ new techniques addressing problems challenging most cultural heritage projects that our current society is encountering.

Upon completing this course, students will:
- -have a general understanding of the subdisciplines of anthropology;
- -know the key concepts of the field;
- -know the major theories that explain human cultural and societal diversity and complexity;
- -be able to use the discipline’s holistic approach to understand human societies and cultures.

This course is designed for students to:
1) learn anthropological perspectives on ethnicity;
2) reflect on their own ethnic identity (or the lack of it) and question their taken-for-granted assumptions regarding ethnicity;
3) allow themselves to apply what they learn in class in real life situations
4) obtain a perspective regarding the relations among politics, history, culture and peoplehood; and
5) have their own answers about what ethnicity is and related questions for further investigation.

Through taking this course, students will:

- -understand how the treatment of death varies cross-culturally, and thus how our own personal understandings of death are not necessarily universally shared;
- -understand processes of grieving and structures of death ritual cross-culturally;
- -acquire a broad sense of how understandings of death have shifted over human cultural evolution;
- -comprehend how death and fear of death affects contemporary politics, economics, and culture;
- -comprehend more deeply the place of death within our own lives

Students will
1. understand how culture shapes societies’ understandings of the body, love and emotions;
2. comprehend how culturual, social, political, historical, and commercial aspects of body, love and emotions are lined and interrelated;
3. be able to use ethnographic approaches to study of the body and emotions, in particular the value of participant-observation fieldwork methodologies;
4. understand how the study of the body, love, and emotions contributes to central anthropological concepts, frameworks, debates and theories;
5. be able to discuss significant issues concerning corporeal and emotional life in their own culture so that they can better comprehend how local identities are formed and contested in a globalizing world.

Students taking this course will:
1) learn to see the cultural nature of the economy and of power;
2) be able to understand how economic behavior that seems irrational in market capitalist contexts can make sense in other cultural contexts;
3) learn how to combine universalizing theories and ethnographic understanding of particular cultures;
4) understand the way the culture of capitalism shapes the way we see the world and affects our behavior;
5) be aware of the history of ecological degradation, consumerism, globalization, nationalism, and fundamentalist religions, and see how they are related to each other.

Upon completing this course, students will be able to:
1. analyze the globalization of culture, a process taking place across the world today and shaping all of our lives;
2. recognize different aspects of the globalization of culture, including those involving recent history, economics, consumption, mass media, migration, and cultural identity;
3. discuss the costs and benefits of contemporary processes of globalization to individuals, societies, and the world; and
4. evaluate the nature of your own culture and selves within a globalizing world.

Students taking this course will:
1) learn anthropological perspectives on gender;
2) reflect on their own gender identity and question their taken-for-granted assumptions regarding ethnicity;
3) understand how gender intersects with other forms of differences;
4) be able to apply what they learn in class in real life situations
5) practice how to inquire with a gendered perspective, and develop their own gender-related questions for further investigation

At the end of the course, students should be able to:
1) Understand the roles and management of museums;
2) Think critically about museum exhibitions and other cultural events;
3) Develop acute senses for art appreciation;
4) Develop interests in culture and history; and
5) Get prepared for career related to management of museums and cultural industries.

Students will:
• know how to design a research project;
• be able to write a proposal to seek funding for a research project;
• understand the ethical challenges and obligations in doing ethnographic research;
• be able to use the major data collecting methods in anthropology.
